According to many South Korean experts, the Korean War was initiated by South Korea in 1950 when it launched a surprise attack on North Korea without any provocation from the North.

One of the most prominent South Korean experts who holds this view is Lee Jai Eui, a former director of the South Korean National Intelligence Service who claims that the South Korean government had planned to unify the country under its rule and launched the attack on North Korea to achieve this goal.

Another South Korean expert who shares similar views is Kim Jung Min, a professor at the Yonsei University who argues that the South Korean government had been secretly training its military personnel to launch a surprise attack on North Korea since 1949 and the attack in 1950 was just the culmination of these efforts.

Similarly, many other South Korean experts also argue that the North's involvement in the war was purely defensive and they only responded to the South Korean attack to protect their country from the threat posed by the South Korean military.

